import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put("Apple", 1);
        map.put("Banana", 2);

        // ❌ Throws ConcurrentModificationException
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : map.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getKey().equals("Apple")) {
                map.remove(entry.getKey()); 
            }
        }
    }
}


The Right Ways: How to Fix It

1. Use Iterator.remove() (Single-Threaded Best Practice)---------------

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Map;

Iterator<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> iterator = map.entrySet().iterator();
while (iterator.hasNext()) {
    Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry = iterator.next();
    if (entry.getKey().equals("Apple")) {
        iterator.remove(); //  Safe and clean
    }
}


2. Use removeIf() (Modern Java 8+ Way)
//  Safely removes elements matching the condition
map.entrySet().removeIf(entry -> entry.getKey().equals("Apple"));



3. Use ConcurrentHashMap (Multi-Threaded Environment)

If multiple threads are reading and writing to your map at the same time, a regular HashMap is not safe. You should switch to ConcurrentHashMap.
ConcurrentHashMap uses a fail-safe iterator. It operates on a view/weekly-consistent copy of the data, meaning it never throws a ConcurrentModificationException, even if another thread modifies it while you iterate.
java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

//  Safe to modify during iteration, even across multiple threads
Map<String, Integer> concurrentMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
concurrentMap.put("Apple", 1);
concurrentMap.put("Banana", 2);

for (String key : concurrentMap.keySet()) {
    if (key.equals("Apple")) {
        concurrentMap.remove(key); //  No exception thrown!
    }



# Fail-Fast vs Fail-Safe Iterators in Java

A quick reference guide comparing the core behavioral differences between **Fail-Fast** and **Fail-Safe** iterators in Java collections.

---

## Quick Summary

| Feature | Fail-Fast Iterators (`HashMap`) | Fail-Safe Iterators (`ConcurrentHashMap`) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Exception Throws** | Throws `ConcurrentModificationException` instantly if modified. | Never throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. |
| **Data Copy** | Operates directly on the original collection. | Operates on a clone or a weakly consistent view. |
| **Memory Overhead** | Extremely low memory overhead. | Higher memory overhead due to tracking views/clones. |
| **Use Case** | Single-threaded applications. | Concurrent / Multi-threaded applications. |

---

## Detailed Breakdown

### 1. Fail-Fast Iterators
* **Behavior:** These iterators immediately abort operation and throw an exception if the underlying collection is structurally modified (added, updated, or deleted) after the iterator is created.
* **Mechanism:** They look at an internal modification counter (`modCount`). If a change is detected during traversal, they flag it instantly.
* **Examples:** `HashMap`, `ArrayList`, `HashSet`, `Vector`.

### 2. Fail-Safe / Weakly Consistent Iterators
* **Behavior:** These iterators allow the collection to be structurally modified during traversal without interrupting the process.
* **Mechanism:** They avoid conflicting with ongoing operations by iterating over a separate snapshot copy or using a weakly consistent view of the dataset.
* **Examples:** `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CopyOnWriteArrayList`.



